[{ "action": "append", "id": "event-modals", "value" : "<div tabindex=\"-1\" id=\"modal-c4f852e7-7526-443b-af90-70ecaf91f669\" class=\"modal\"><div class=\"modal-dialog modal-lg\"><div class=\"modal-content\"><div class=\"modal-header\"><a data-dismiss=\"modal\" class=\"close\"><span class=\"glyphicon glyphicon-remove\"></span></a><p class=\"text-muted\"><span class=\"glyphicon glyphicon-road\"></span> Research Track</p><strong>Fri 2 May 2025 16:30 - 16:45 at <a href=\"https://conf.researchr.org/room/icse-2025/icse-2025-venue-210\" class=\"room-link navigate\">210</a></strong> - <a href=\"https://conf.researchr.org/track/icse-2025/icse-2025-research-track#program\" class=\"navigate\">Security and QA</a> <small>Chair(s): <a href=\"https://conf.researchr.org/profile/icse-2025/nafisehkahani1\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"navigate\">Nafiseh Kahani</a></small></div><div class=\"modal-body\"><div class=\"bg-primary event-title\"><h4><span data-event-star=\"c4f852e7-7526-443b-af90-70ecaf91f669\" title=\"Add event to your program\"><span class=\"text-disabled glyphicon glyphicon-star-empty\"></span></span><strong>Early Detection of Performance Regressions by Bridging Local Performance Data and Architectural Models</strong></h4></div><div class=\"bg-info event-description\"><span class=\"pull-right\"><span class=\"output-badge\"><span class=\"label-primary label\">Security</span></span></span><span class=\"pull-right\"><span title=\"This paper won an award as a distinguished paper\" class=\"output-badge\"><span class=\"label-primary label\">Award Winner</span></span></span><p>During software development, developers often make numerous modifications to the software to address existing issues or implement new features. However, certain changes may inadvertently have a detrimental impact on the overall system performance. To ensure that the performance of new software releases does not degrade (i.e., absence of performance regressions), existing practices rely on system-level performance testing, such as load testing, or component-level performance testing, such as microbenchmarking, to detect performance regressions. However, performance testing for the entire system is often expensive and time-consuming, posing challenges to adapting to the rapid release cycles common in modern DevOps practices. In addition, system-level performance testing cannot be conducted until the system is fully built and deployed. On the other hand, component-level testing focuses on isolated components, neglecting overall system performance and the impact of system workloads. In this paper, we propose a novel approach to early detection of performance regressions by bridging the local performance data generated by component-level testing and the system-level architectural models. Our approach uses local performance data to identify deviations at the component level, and then propagate these deviations to the architectural model. We then use the architectural model to predict regressions in the performance of the overall system. In an evaluation of our approach on two representative open-source benchmark systems, we show that it can effectively detect end-to-end system performance regressions from local performance deviations with different intensities and under various system workloads. More importantly, our approach can detect regressions as early as in the development phase, in contrast to existing approaches that require the system to be fully built and deployed. Our approach is lightweight and can complement traditional system performance testing when testing resources are scarce.</p>\n<p></p><p></p><div class=\"row\"><div class=\"col-sm-6\"><a href=\"https://conf.researchr.org/profile/icse-2025/lizhiliao\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"navigate\"><div class=\"media\"><span class=\"pull-left\"><img alt=\"Lizhi Liao\" src=\"https://conf.researchr.org/getProfileImage/lizhiliao/f1e2de7b-1ac2-48f5-8bd1-22868b3a0b49/micro-avatar?1783973088000\" class=\"outputimage media-object thumbnail no-bottom-margin\"/></span><div class=\"media-body\"><h5 class=\"media-heading\">Lizhi Liao<span class=\"pull-right\"><small></small></span></h5><h5 class=\"media-heading\"><span class=\"text-black\">Memorial University of Newfoundland</span></h5><h5 class=\"media-heading\"><small>Canada</small></h5></div></div></a></div><div class=\"col-sm-6\"><a href=\"https://conf.researchr.org/profile/icse-2025/simoneismann\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"navigate\"><div class=\"media\"><span class=\"pull-left\"><img alt=\"micro-avatar\" src=\"https://conf.researchr.org/getDefaultImage/micro-avatar?1783985080000\" class=\"outputimage media-object thumbnail no-bottom-margin\"/></span><div class=\"media-body\"><h5 class=\"media-heading\">Simon Eismann<span class=\"pull-right\"><small></small></span></h5><h5 class=\"media-heading\"><span class=\"text-black\">University of W\u00FCrzburg</span></h5></div></div></a></div></div><div class=\"row\"><div class=\"col-sm-6\"><a href=\"https://conf.researchr.org/profile/icse-2025/hengli\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"navigate\"><div class=\"media\"><span class=\"pull-left\"><img alt=\"Heng Li\" src=\"https://conf.researchr.org/getProfileImage/hengli/0a4161b6-9462-440f-bbc0-8490d87b7c42/micro-avatar?1783973787000\" class=\"outputimage media-object thumbnail no-bottom-margin\"/></span><div class=\"media-body\"><h5 class=\"media-heading\">Heng Li<span class=\"pull-right\"><small></small></span></h5><h5 class=\"media-heading\"><span class=\"text-black\">Polytechnique Montr\u00E9al</span></h5><h5 class=\"media-heading\"><small>Canada</small></h5></div></div></a></div><div class=\"col-sm-6\"><a href=\"https://conf.researchr.org/profile/icse-2025/corpaulbezemer\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"navigate\"><div class=\"media\"><span class=\"pull-left\"><img alt=\"Cor-Paul Bezemer\" src=\"https://conf.researchr.org/getProfileImage/corpaulbezemer/628705ab-d0db-43b3-9095-32c37814ea82/micro-avatar?1783974012000\" class=\"outputimage media-object thumbnail no-bottom-margin\"/></span><div class=\"media-body\"><h5 class=\"media-heading\">Cor-Paul Bezemer<span class=\"pull-right\"><small></small></span></h5><h5 class=\"media-heading\"><span class=\"text-black\">University of Alberta</span></h5><h5 class=\"media-heading\"><small>Canada</small></h5></div></div></a></div></div><div class=\"row\"><div class=\"col-sm-6\"><a href=\"https://conf.researchr.org/profile/icse-2025/diegocosta\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"navigate\"><div class=\"media\"><span class=\"pull-left\"><img alt=\"Diego Elias Costa\" src=\"https://conf.researchr.org/getProfileImage/diegocosta/fa3ae852-c49c-4c6c-9844-a1be427e5b11/micro-avatar?1783975748000\" class=\"outputimage media-object thumbnail no-bottom-margin\"/></span><div class=\"media-body\"><h5 class=\"media-heading\">Diego Elias <span class=\"name-visual-sep\"></span>Costa<span class=\"pull-right\"><small></small></span></h5><h5 class=\"media-heading\"><span class=\"text-black\">Concordia University, Canada</span></h5><h5 class=\"media-heading\"><small>Canada</small></h5></div></div></a></div><div class=\"col-sm-6\"><a href=\"https://conf.researchr.org/profile/icse-2025/andrevanhoorn\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"navigate\"><div class=\"media\"><span class=\"pull-left\"><img alt=\"Andr&eacute; van Hoorn\" src=\"https://conf.researchr.org/getProfileImage/andrevanhoorn/b064d044-43aa-46b1-ab34-26556b2a2a90/micro-avatar?1783972816000\" class=\"outputimage media-object thumbnail no-bottom-margin\"/></span><div class=\"media-body\"><h5 class=\"media-heading\">Andr\u00E9 <span class=\"name-visual-sep\"></span>van Hoorn<span class=\"pull-right\"><small></small></span></h5><h5 class=\"media-heading\"><span class=\"text-black\">University of Hamburg, Germany</span></h5><h5 class=\"media-heading\"><small>Germany</small></h5></div></div></a></div></div><div class=\"row\"><div class=\"col-sm-6\"><a href=\"https://conf.researchr.org/profile/icse-2025/weiyiianshang\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"navigate\">